@prajwal1210 to be honest, I'm doubtful that this is causing performance issues for you. What makes you think this? As far as I know, that would happen only if multiple streams get aliased to the same "hardware channel" or "connection", in which case you get false dependencies, since you are sharing queues. By default, there are 8 hardware channels. So this would happen only if you have 8 streams running concurrently in your cuda graph and your entire workload at the same exact time. I don't see how this can happen with NCCL, but could be wrong (and would be happy to be proven wrong if you have an example).

You can try increasing CUDA_DEVICE_MAX_CONNECTIONS as a workaround if you really do have this problem.

https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-c-programming-guide/index.html#cuda-environment-variables

